1043	/// Indicates whether an aggregation function is monotonic as a set
1044	/// function. A set function is monotonically increasing if its value
1045	/// increases as its argument grows (as a set). Formally, `f` is a
1046	/// monotonically increasing set function if `f(S) >= f(T)` whenever `S`
1047	/// is a superset of `T`.
1048	///
1049	/// For example `COUNT` and `MAX` are monotonically increasing as their
1050	/// values always increase (or stay the same) as new values are seen. On
1051	/// the other hand, `MIN` is monotonically decreasing as its value always
1052	/// decreases or stays the same as new values are seen.
1053	#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq)]
1054	pub enum SetMonotonicity {
1055	    /// Aggregate value increases or stays the same as the input set grows.
1056	    Increasing,
1057	    /// Aggregate value decreases or stays the same as the input set grows.
1058	    Decreasing,
1059	    /// Aggregate value may increase, decrease, or stay the same as the input
1060	    /// set grows.
1061	    NotMonotonic,
1062	}
